# Simulating a tank filling at a rate: each time round a repeat is a minute, a pipe adds its litres,
# a leak takes some away, and a repeat until stops when the tank is full. The mill's tank at the foot
# of the waterfall in the gorge.
lesson coding-simulating-a-tank-filling v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=4 subject=coding levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Simulating a tank filling"
  goal "Simulate a tank filling at a rate, minute by minute, and work out how long it takes to fill."

  look {
    scene 36x29 {
      program p code=["set water to 0", "set minutes to 0", "repeat until water is at least 60", "  add 12 to water", "  take 2 from water", "  add 1 to minutes"] text=true at=canvas(0, 15)
      waterfall wf drops=2 rainbow=1 at=canvas(0, 0)
      waterwheel ww paddles=8 right-of=wf gap=2
    }
    say """
      A pipe from the waterfall fills the mill's tank, which holds 60 litres. In the program each
      time round the repeat is one minute: the pipe adds 12 litres and a crack lets 2 out, so the
      tank gains 10 a minute. minutes counts the times round, and the repeat stops as soon as the
      water is at least 60. The program says it takes 6 minutes.
      """
  }
  do {
    show coding.tank-fill n=5 r=8 easy-n=4 easy-r=5 hard-n=6 hard-r=15
    level easy {
      worked coding.tank-fill n=3 r=8
    }
    show coding.tank-until c=100 r=12 easy-c=80 easy-r=10 hard-c=80 hard-r=15
    show coding.tank-leak c=72 r=15 l=6 easy-c=60 easy-l=3 hard-c=90
    show coding.tank-trace r=12 l=3 j=1 easy-r=10 easy-l=4 easy-j=0 hard-r=15 hard-l=4 hard-j=2
    show coding.tank-graph g=8 l=2 easy-g=6 easy-l=1 hard-g=12 hard-l=4
    show coding.tank-switch n=7 a=3 f=15 s=5 easy-n=6 easy-a=2 easy-f=20 hard-n=8 hard-a=4 hard-f=20 hard-s=4
    show coding.tank-until c=80 r=12 easy-c=120 hard-c=100 hard-r=15
    show coding.tank-leak c=96 r=18 l=2 easy-c=72 easy-r=12 easy-l=3 hard-c=72 hard-r=12 hard-l=6
    level hard {
      show coding.tank-graph g=10 l=4
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show coding.tank-double k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show coding.tank-slowest k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "In a simulation each time round stands for a step of time, and what the tank gains in a step is what comes in less what goes out."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The program is a model of a real tank: one time round is one minute, the pipe adds its litres,
    the crack takes some away, and a repeat until stops when the tank is full. The common mistakes
    are adding the leak instead of taking it away, stopping one minute early when the rate does not
    share the tank's size exactly, and counting the first minute of a counter that starts at 0 as
    minute 1. In the child's working, look for a column of litres, one for each minute. If the child
    is stuck, fill a jug from a tap with a cup at a time and take a spoonful out after each cup, and
    count the cups. Off the page, time how long a basin takes to fill with the plug half in. The
    two-star question tests the claim that twice the pipe takes half the time; the three-star
    question works backwards to the slowest pipe that fills the tank in time.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the pipe brings 5, 8, 10 or 12 litres, one tank is worked in the teacher's pen, every rate shares the tank exactly, the leaks are 2 or 3 litres, the table is read at the end of the first minute, the graph's leak is 1 or 2 litres, and the sluice is open for two minutes."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the rates do not share the tank exactly, so the last minute runs over, the leaks are up to 6 litres, the table is read in the middle of a minute, the graph has a leak to put back, the sluice closes after four minutes to a trickle of 4, and a second graph is added."
  }
}
